-- title:
--   §2.4 — Q is convex, h·P{D<y} − b·P{D≥y} is its left derivative and a subgradient, and E[H] equals it
-- statement:
--   Let demand $D \ge 0$ have law $P$ with finite mean, let $h, b > 0$, and let
--   $$Q(y) = h\,E[y-D]^+ + b\,E[D-y]^+, \qquad g(y) = h\,P\{D < y\} - b\,P\{D \ge y\}.$$
--   Then:
--
--   1. $Q$ is convex on $[0,\infty)$;
--   2. for every $y > 0$, $g(y)$ is the left derivative of $Q$ at $y$;
--   3. $g(y)$ is a subgradient of $Q$ on $[0,\infty)$ at every $y \ge 0$:
--   $$Q(w) \ge Q(y) + g(y)\,(w-y) \qquad \text{for all } w \ge 0;$$
--   4. the censored estimate $H(y) = h$ if $D < y$, $H(y) = -b$ if $D \ge y$, of Eq. (5) satisfies $E[H(y)] = g(y)$ for every level $y$;
--   5. along the AIM run (either regime, any parameters), the estimate is conditionally unbiased given the current target: for every $t \ge 1$, $$E\big[H_t(\hat y_t) \,\big|\, \hat y_t\big] = g(\hat y_t) \quad \text{almost surely},$$ where $H_t(\hat y_t) = h$ if $D_t < \hat y_t$ and $-b$ otherwise, and the conditioning is on the σ-algebra generated by $\hat y_t$;
--   6. $|H(y)| \le \max\{b,h\}$.
--
--   These are the facts §2.4 uses to apply Lemma 3 to $\Phi = Q$ on $S = [0,\bar y]$ with $\bar B = \max\{b,h\}$. Item 5 holds because $\hat y_t$ depends only on $D_1,\dots,D_{t-1}$ and $D_t$ is independent of them.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Eq. (2) on p. 6 calls $\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}(Q(y+\varepsilon)-Q(y))/\varepsilon = h P[y \ge D] - b P[y < D]$ a "left-derivative"; that limit is the right derivative. The left derivative is the one stated in §2.4, and it is what $H_t$ estimates; both are subgradients. The left derivative is stated as `HasDerivWithinAt` on $(-\infty, y)$. In item 5 the demand path is zero-based, so period $t+1$ uses $D_{t+1} = $ `d t` and the target `aimTarget … d (t + 1)`. The positivity of $h$, $b$ is implicit in the paper.
-- source:
--   Huh & Rusmevichientong, A Non-Parametric Asymptotic Analysis of Inventory Planning with Censored Demand, Math. Oper. Res. (2009), author's manuscript, p. 10, §2.4 (with Eqs. (1)–(2), p. 6, and Eq. (5), p. 7)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AIMInventory_Known_AIM
open MeasureTheory
open scoped NNReal

namespace AIMInventory.Known

/-- §2.4, p. 10 (with (1)–(2), p. 6): `Q` is convex on `[0, ∞)`, its left derivative at `y > 0`
is `h·P{D < y} − b·P{D ≥ y}`, this value is a subgradient of `Q` on `[0, ∞)` at every `y ≥ 0`, the
censored estimate `H` of (5) has this value as its mean, along the AIM run
`E[H_t(ŷ_t) | ŷ_t] = h·P{D_1 < ŷ_t} − b·P{D_1 ≥ ŷ_t}` almost surely (period `t + 1` uses the
demand `D_{t+1} = d t` and the target `ŷ_{t+1}`), and `|H| ≤ max{b, h}`. -/
theorem subgradient_left_derivative (P : CappedBaseStock.DemandLaw) (h b : ℝ)
    (hh : 0 < h) (hb : 0 < b) :
    ConvexOn ℝ (Set.Ici 0) (Q P h b) ∧
    (∀ y : ℝ, 0 < y → HasDerivWithinAt (Q P h b) (leftDeriv P h b y) (Set.Iio y) y) ∧
    (∀ y w : ℝ, 0 ≤ y → 0 ≤ w → Q P h b y + leftDeriv P h b y * (w - y) ≤ Q P h b w) ∧
    (∀ y : ℝ, ∫ D : ℝ≥0, gradEst h b (D : ℝ) y ∂P.law = leftDeriv P h b y) ∧
    (∀ (r : Regime) (ybar γ yhat1 : ℝ) (t : ℕ),
      (CappedBaseStock.demandPathLaw P)[fun d =>
          gradEst h b (d t : ℝ) (aimTarget r ybar γ b h yhat1 d (t + 1)) |
        MeasurableSpace.comap (fun d => aimTarget r ybar γ b h yhat1 d (t + 1)) inferInstance]
        =ᵐ[CappedBaseStock.demandPathLaw P]
          fun d => leftDeriv P h b (aimTarget r ybar γ b h yhat1 d (t + 1))) ∧
    (∀ D y : ℝ, |gradEst h b D y| ≤ max b h) := by sorry

end AIMInventory.Known
